<BaseLayout title="Crystallography" description="Lattices, unit cells, reciprocal space, diffraction, space groups, and Wyckoff positions for periodic electronic structure." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Crystallography</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Crystallography</h1>
  <p class="lede">Crystallography supplies the geometric language of periodic matter: lattices, cells, coordinates, reciprocal space, symmetry operations, space groups, Wyckoff positions, and diffraction.</p>
  <p>Electronic-structure calculations inherit these choices. A structure file is not merely a list of atoms; it encodes a periodic model, coordinate convention, symmetry description, and finite numerical cell.</p>

  <h2>A crystal combines a lattice with a basis</h2>
  <p>A Bravais lattice is generated by integer translations of primitive vectors:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="R equals n one a one plus n two a two plus n three a three">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;=n_1\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_1+n_2\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_2+n_3\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_3,\quad n_i\in\mathbb&#123;Z&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The basis is the set of atoms or motifs attached to each lattice point. Two structures may share the same Bravais lattice but differ in basis, chemistry, symmetry, and electronic properties.</p>

  <h2>Primitive and conventional cells serve different purposes</h2>
  <p>A primitive cell contains one lattice point and is often computationally economical. A conventional cell may be larger but makes crystal-system axes, centering, and symmetry easier to recognize.</p>
  <p>Different cells can describe the same infinite crystal. Energies, k-point meshes, band labels, phonon wavevectors, and atom counts must therefore be compared after accounting for cell transformations and normalization per atom, formula unit, primitive cell, or volume.</p>

  <h2>Fractional and Cartesian coordinates must not be mixed</h2>
  <p>A fractional coordinate <math class="math-inline" aria-label="x one x two x three"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_1,x_2,x_3)</annotation></semantics></math> means</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="r equals x one a one plus x two a two plus x three a three">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;=x_1\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_1+x_2\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_2+x_3\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_3.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Changing the cell without transforming fractional coordinates changes the physical structure. Changing settings, origins, or axis order can alter the printed coordinates while leaving the crystal equivalent.</p>

  <h2>The reciprocal lattice is dual to translation</h2>
  <p>Reciprocal primitive vectors are conventionally defined by</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="a i dot b j equals two pi delta i j">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_i\cdot\mathbf&#123;b&#125;_j=2\pi\delta_&#123;ij&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The factor of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="two pi"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is conventional and must match the Fourier convention. Reciprocal vectors describe periodic Fourier components, diffraction conditions, Brillouin zones, and crystal-momentum equivalence.</p>

  <h2>Miller indices label families of lattice planes</h2>
  <p>Integers <math class="math-inline" aria-label="h k l"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(hkl)</annotation></semantics></math> specify a reciprocal-lattice direction and the orientation of a family of direct-space planes. Their metric spacing depends on the lattice; the same indices do not imply the same physical spacing in different cells.</p>
  <p>Plane indices, crystallographic directions, reciprocal vectors, and Cartesian vectors are related but distinct objects. Brackets and coordinate conventions carry meaning.</p>

  <h2>Diffraction measures periodic Fourier content</h2>
  <p>For atoms at positions <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r j"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_j</annotation></semantics></math>, a schematic structure factor is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="F of G equals sum j f j exponential minus i G dot r j">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi><mo></mo></munderover><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf&#123;G&#125;)=\sum_j f_j e^&#123;-i\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_j&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Systematic absences can result from translational symmetry and basis interference. A refined structural model is inferred from measured intensities through experimental and statistical procedures; a DFT-relaxed structure is not automatically an experimentally determined crystal structure.</p>

  <h2>Space groups combine point operations with translations</h2>
  <p>Rotations, reflections, inversion, screw axes, glide planes, and translations form the symmetry of a periodic crystal. The space-group label depends on a conventional setting and origin choice; the symmetry operations are the operative content.</p>
  <p>A numerical structure can appear to lose symmetry because of coordinate rounding or relaxation tolerances. Conversely, forcing a high-symmetry model can suppress a real distortion, magnetic order, charge order, or soft mode.</p>

  <h2>Wyckoff positions classify symmetry-related sites</h2>
  <p>A Wyckoff position records multiplicity and site symmetry within a space group. Atoms occupying the same Wyckoff orbit are generated from one representative by space-group operations.</p>
  <p>Wyckoff labels are setting-dependent. They are not chemical oxidation states, orbital labels, or guarantees that two sites remain electronically equivalent after magnetism, spin–orbit coupling, defects, surfaces, or symmetry breaking are introduced.</p>

  <h2>Structure standardization is useful but not neutral provenance</h2>
  <p>Transforming a CIF or cell into a standard setting can simplify comparison, symmetry analysis, and k-space conventions. It may reorder atoms, change the cell, shift the origin, or select an equivalent setting.</p>
  <p>A reproducible workflow should retain the source structure, transformation matrix, origin shift, symmetry tolerance, software and version, and the standardized result. “Same space group” alone does not prove that two coordinate sets are the same structure.</p>

  <h2>Periodic electronic structure uses only part of crystallography</h2>
  <p>Ground-state calculations need a valid periodic structure and consistent reciprocal conventions. Phonons, magnetic order, defects, surfaces, diffraction comparison, topology, and phase transitions require deeper use of subgroups, irreducible representations, supercells, and subperiodic or magnetic groups.</p>
  <p>High-symmetry band paths are conventions derived from reciprocal-space symmetry. They are not substitutes for full-zone integration or a search for extrema away from the selected path.</p>

  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.iucr.org/education">International Union of Crystallography educational resources</a> provide teaching pamphlets and institutional material spanning lattices, symmetry, diffraction, structure factors, and reciprocal space. The collection is heterogeneous; each pamphlet has its own level and scope.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.cryst.ehu.es/">Bilbao Crystallographic Server</a> is the principal reviewed tool and reference for space groups, Wyckoff positions, subgroups, k-vector types, irreducible representations, compatibility relations, and Brillouin-zone data. It is not a zero-background course, and tool output requires the server's requested citations.</p>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-091sc-introduction-to-solid-state-chemistry-fall-2010/">MIT OpenCourseWare 3.091SC</a> is a broad materials-chemistry entrance that connects bonding, crystal structure, diffraction, defects, and semiconductors. It does not replace a dedicated crystallography reference.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not reproduce the International Tables, teach structure refinement in full, or certify an uploaded structure. It establishes the geometric and symmetry objects that must be specified before periodic electronic-structure calculations can be interpreted.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> IUCr education pages, Bilbao tools, and MIT 3.091SC were reviewed on 3 August 2026. International Tables access and quotation boundaries remain a separate licensing question.</p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
